<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="prep-11-19">
  <title>Resources for 11/19</title>

  <objectives>
    <introduction>
      <p>For the content of this class, you will be expected to be able to do the following:</p>
    </introduction>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          Explain the statement of the <em>Extreme Value Theorem</em>. 
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Explain the maximum and minimum values of the range of a function in terms of the infimum and supremum of the range. 
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Explain how infima and/or suprema are used to prove the Extreme Value Theorem, and thus how this theorem is firmly connected to the Completeness Axiom.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Use the Extreme Value Theorem to prove theorems about continuous functions.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </objectives>

  <subsection xml:id="video-activity-evt">
    <title>Video &amp; Activities for Extreme Value Theorem</title>
    <p>
      Below are links to a video on the <em>Extreme Value Theorem</em>, which is available both on Google Drive and YouTube. It is followed by a link to a set of activities that we will be working on in class. Please watch the video and do as many of the activities as you can before class.
    </p>

    <video youtube="G44ntA-phnY" />

    <example>
      <title>Extreme Value Theorem</title>
      <p>
        Let <m>f: [a,b] \rightarrow \mathbb{R}</m> be a continous function.
      </p>
      <p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              Let <m>B</m> be a bounded set of real numbers, with <m>m = \inf(B)</m> and <m>M= \sup(B)</m>.
            <ol>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Prove that there is a sequence <m>\{ a_n \}</m> with each <m>a_n \in B</m> that converges to <m>m</m>.   Hint:  Look at <m>m+\frac{1}{n}</m>.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Prove that there exists a sequence <m>\{ b_n \}</m> with each <m>b_n \in B</m> that converges to <m>M</m>.  Hint:  Look at <m>M-\frac{1}{n}</m>.
                </p>
              </li>
            </ol>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Assume that <m>f</m> is unbounded from above.
            <ol>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Try to construct a sequence <m>\{ x_n \}</m> such that <m>\{ f(x_n) \}</m> converges to <m>\infty</m>.  Hint: Try to choose <m>x_n</m> to be large in such a way that <m>f(x_n)</m> keeps getting larger as <m>n</m> gets larger.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Is <m>\{ x_n \}</m> bounded?  Does it converge?  Is there a subsequence that converges?
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Let <m>\{ x_{p_n} \}</m> be the convergent subsequence you found in (b).  What does <m>\{ f(x_{p_n}) \}</m> <q>converge</q> to?  What does this tell you?
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  What do you do if <m>f</m> is unbounded from below?
                </p>
              </li>
            </ol>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Let <m>f: [a,b] \rightarrow \mathbb{R}</m> be a continuous function, and let <m>R</m> be the range of <m>f</m>.
            <ol>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Suppose <m>s = \sup(R)</m>.  Prove that there is a sequence <m>\{ s_n \}</m> in <m>R</m> that converges to <m>s</m>.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Prove that there is a sequence <m>\{ x_n \} \subseteq [a,b]</m> such that <m>\{ f(x_n) \}</m> converges to <m>s</m>.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Prove there is a subsequence of <m>\{ x_n \}</m> (say <m>\{ x_{p_n} \}</m>) that converges to some <m>x</m>.  What is <m>f(x)</m>?
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  If <m>\inf(R)</m> exists, prove that <m>\inf(R) \in R</m>.
                </p>
              </li>
            </ol>
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </example>
    <p>
      Feel free to post any questions you have on the <url href="https://www.figma.com/board/aJvcTlf1GEiG9CPhI3Vh3h/Notes-for-8-31--Class-Introduction----408--First-Day-Template-?t=ZdQWknSkCn3PfyKQ-6">FigJam board</url> for the next class.
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection>
    <title>Optional Reading</title>
    <p>
      Section 5.7: pp. 326-327
    </p>
  </subsection>
</section>